@database "basic_operations"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: basic_operations.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Basic Operations"
@{h1}Basic Operations

Two small tools for agents. @{b}wait@{ub} pauses for a number of seconds --
for a build, a deployment or a rate limit to pass -- and shows a countdown
in the chat's status line; for a long pause it can instead end the turn and
have the session woken when the time is up. @{b}ping@{ub} answers "Pong!"
with the server's time, a quick check that the tool path works. The plugin
keeps no data and has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The wait tool  " link wait}
  @{"  Waking instead of waiting  " link wake}
  @{"  The ping tool  " link ping}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    basic_operations:
      type: basic_operations
      enabled: true
      max_wait_seconds: 3600
      default_update_interval: 5.0
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+basic_operations/*"
@{body}

It needs nothing beyond the core requirements. The settings, read from the
server entry when the plugin starts:

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
max_wait_seconds | 3600 | the longest wait accepted, also shown to the model as the maximum of seconds
default_update_interval | 1.0 | how long the wait loop sleeps between looks at the clock; it must be above 0, or every wait answers "Update interval must be positive"
@{body}

The interval does not set how often the status line changes (every 10
seconds, fixed) nor how fast a stop is noticed (within a second, fixed); in
practice it changes nothing a user sees. The model cannot set it.

Waking a session (see @{" Waking instead of waiting " link wake}) needs
session presence, @{tt}session_presence.enabled@{utt} in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}. The plugin stores nothing on disk.

@{h2}Running it on its own

@{code}
python -m plugins.basic_operations --operation ping --include-details
python -m plugins.basic_operations --operation wait --seconds 5 --message "build"
python -m plugins.basic_operations --server --port 9010
@{body}

Run from @{tt}src/@{utt} (or with it on @{tt}PYTHONPATH@{utt}). The first
two call a tool once and print the answer. @{tt}--server@{utt} serves both
tools over HTTP on 127.0.0.1 (default port 9010); calls need the key from
the @{tt}HTTP_SERVER_AUTH_KEY@{utt} environment variable when it is set.
@endnode

@node wait "The wait tool"
@{h1}The wait tool

@{tt}basic_operations_wait@{utt} in the default setup (the instance name
plus @{tt}_wait@{utt}). It holds the agent's turn until the time is up; the
chat shows a countdown meanwhile, and the user's stop ends it within a
second.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
seconds | required; any number above 0 up to max_wait_seconds (the schema says from 0.1, the code takes less). A number as text ("30") is read too
message | what the status line calls the wait; default "Waiting" (also for null or empty). Cut to 100 characters
wake | true: do not hold the turn, wake the session when the time is up (see @{" Waking instead of waiting " link wake}); default false. The text "false" is false
@{body}

@{h2}Answers

A finished wait:

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "message": "Wait completed successfully",
 "requested_seconds": 30.0, "actual_seconds": 30.004, "user_message": "build"}
@{body}

When @{tt}wake@{utt} was asked for but the call waited here after all, the
answer adds @{tt}wake_note@{utt} with the reason (see
@{" Waking instead of waiting " link wake}).

Stopped by the user: @{tt}status@{utt} "cancelled", @{tt}cancelled@{utt}
true, @{tt}actual_seconds@{utt} how long it had waited (0 when stopped
before it began), plus @{tt}requested_seconds@{utt} and
@{tt}user_message@{utt}.

Refused, as @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt}:

@{table}
error | When
seconds must be a number above 0 | seconds is 0 or negative, missing, null, NaN, text that is not a number, a list or an object
Wait time exceeds maximum of 3600.0 seconds | seconds above max_wait_seconds, infinity included
Update interval must be positive | default_update_interval in the server entry is 0 or below
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"seconds": 45, "message": "waiting for the deployment"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node wake "Waking instead of waiting"
@{h1}Waking instead of waiting

A wait holds the turn: the agent does nothing else and the run stays open.
With @{tt}wake: true@{utt} and @{tt}seconds@{utt} of 60 or more, the call
answers at once instead:

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "waiting": true, "requested_seconds": 600.0,
 "user_message": "build", "note": "end your turn now -- you are woken in 600 s. ..."}
@{body}

The agent ends its turn; when the time is up the session is woken and a
new turn starts. The wake carries no text of its own -- the message appears
in the server log ("the wait is over: build"), not in the woken turn. Below
60 seconds a wake is not armed: the woken turn reads the whole conversation
again, which costs more than waiting.

When the session cannot be woken, the call waits here as without
@{tt}wake@{utt} and says why in @{tt}wake_note@{utt}:

@{table}
wake_note | Why
a wake needs at least 60 s | seconds below 60
session presence is off (config: session_presence.enabled) | waking is not configured
this call has no session to wake | a call without a session
this call belongs to no session, so there is nobody to wake | a call without a user
this run was itself woken and ends with its turn | the run is a wake itself; a wake does not arm the next
waking is switched off (config: session_presence.max_wake_depth is 0) | waking off by setting
a sub-agent's session is never woken | the caller is a sub-agent; its parent run takes its result
@{body}

@{h2}The limit

An armed wake is a sleeping task in the process that took the call,
nothing on disk. A one-shot @{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} ends with its turn,
and a restart or plugin reload before the time is up drops the wake (the
log says so). Then nobody rings, and the session sits where it ended its
turn. For a pause that must survive that, wait without @{tt}wake@{utt}.
@endnode

@node ping "The ping tool"
@{h1}The ping tool

@{tt}basic_operations_ping@{utt}: shows that a tool call reaches the server
and comes back.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
include_details | true adds details (below); default false. The text "false" is false
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "message": "Pong!",
 "timestamp": "2026-09-30T07:19:17.570+02:00", "server_name": "basic_operations"}
@{body}

@{tt}timestamp@{utt} is the server's local time in ISO 8601, to the
millisecond, with its UTC offset.
@{tt}details@{utt} holds @{tt}python_version@{utt}, @{tt}platform@{utt} and
@{tt}server_config@{utt} (the two settings from
@{" Setting it up " link setup}).
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The descriptions and parameters come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, about 1,250 characters for both tools, sent with
every request. The maximum of @{tt}seconds@{utt} is filled in from
@{tt}max_wait_seconds@{utt}. Nothing checks the arguments against the
schema before the call; the code takes what the tables in
@{" The wait tool " link wait} and @{" The ping tool " link ping} say.

@{h2}The status line

@{table}
When | Line
wait begins | build: starting countdown - 30.0s
every 10 seconds | build: 20.0s remaining
wait done | Waited 30.0s of 30.0s -- build
stopped | build: cancelled after 12.3s
wake armed | build: waking the session in 600.0s
ping | Pong from basic_operations at 2026-09-30T07:19:17.570+02:00
@{body}

A refused call shows its error text.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

An answer is a few hundred characters (ping with details a little more),
appended to the end of the conversation like any other tool answer. The
plugin has no hook and adds nothing to the system prompt, so the cached
prompt stays intact. A plain wait costs no tokens while it runs; a wake
costs a whole turn, which is why it needs 60 seconds or more.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A wake is lost on a restart, and never happens from a one-shot
agent-cli run (see @{" Waking instead of waiting " link wake}).
@{bullet}The woken turn is not told which wait woke it.
@endnode
